eland  the temple that is the house of God was  destroyed and in exile they were forced  to sort of Forge this new identity and  so  these two texts also very much reflect  that kind of environment this new kind  of thinking the exile period is  incredibly important for the development  of Judaism as we know it it is here that  things like personal prayer and many of  the rituals that we associate with  Judaism comes about as a result of the  drastic change in environments and by  living as a minority group it is likely  that important theological changes were  made at this time as well much  archaeological findings show that in the  Land of Israel and Judah there were many  other gods and idols that were worshiped  alongside Yahweh this is that some  historians to argue that the Israelite  religion pre exile wasn't monotheistic  in the regular sense of the word but  that they practiced manometry are a kind  of Hino theism that is the acceptance  and belief in many different gods but  worshiping only one or considering one  to be more exalted than the others it is  less interesting to see how Judaism  seems to evolve gradually from a  religion much more similar to those of  the other empires at the time that were  mostly polytheistic to eventually become  something very different first through a  kind of Hino theism and then the during  the Babylonian exile especially there is  the shift it seems at least to have very  much more pure monotheism that were then  characterized Judaism going forward from  the time after the Babylonian exile  there is no longer any trace of  monotheism in this culture which seems  to imply that something very important  happened here there was a very important  shift now the Bible itself states that  Judaism was always monotheistic and that  the people over different periods of  time sort of turned away from the true  religion and let us explain it that way  whether you believe this version of the  story or that of historians they both  